  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 236 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nPRECIPITATION CHANCES: \n\nTODAY--THURSDAY...Off-and-on scattered to numerous showers and \nthunderstorms along with areas of drizzle will continue to impact \nthe region today and tonight, gradually exiting to the east \nThursday, as a deep upper trough approaching slowly from the west \ninteracts with a nearly stalled deep frontal zone across the region. \nContinued unseasonably rich precipitable waters, strong forcing, and \nmarginal buoyancy should lead to pockets of heavy rainfall and \nlocalized flooding issues. However, with the exception of possibly \nfar SE KS, much of the current flood watch is probably done with \nhigh convective rainfall rates and associated widespread flooding \nconcerns, as this potential has shifted south into mostly Oklahoma \n(per HREF and REFS consensus) where the better instability resides. \nNevertheless, will continue the flood watch as-is for now and let \nthe day shift diagnose radar and model trends further.\n\nSUNDAY NIGHT--MONDAY...A stubborn southwest CONUS upper low is \nexpected to finally eject east-northeast this weekend, possibly \nbring some low precipitation chances to Mid-America Sunday night \ninto Monday. The GFS and associated ensemble mean are more bullish \nwith precipitation chances than other global model guidance, so left \nthe forecast dry for now.\n\nMID-LATE NEXT WEEK...Deterministic and ensemble consensus supports \nanother deep western CONUS upper trough taking shape next week, \nwhich could support another round of showers/thunderstorms across \nthe region by mid-late week. Still quite a bit of uncertainty and \nmodel spread, so stay tuned as we refine forecast details in the \ncoming days.\n\nTEMPERATURES & WIND:\n\nTODAY-THURSDAY...Had to undercut NBM high temperatures once again \ntoday and Thursday, and probably not quite enough, due to widespread \nclouds/precipitation and the shallow cool airmass spreading south \nacross the region (which the NBM does not handle well). That said, \nthinking central KS will stay in the 50s today, with 70s likely over \nfar southeast KS. Thursday temperatures should be fairly similar to \ntoday. Stout/gusty north winds will expand southeast across the \nregion Thursday in wake of this strong storm system.\n\nFRIDAY-SATURDAY...A deepening central to eastern North American \nupper trough will drive strong Canadian high pressure south into Mid-\nAmerica, supporting the coolest air thus far this fall season. High \ntemperatures in the 50s to lows 60s appear likely. Additionally, \nstout/gusty north winds Friday will make it feel even cooler. The \ncoolest night will be Friday night, with lows likely dipping into \nthe 30s to low 40s underneath the strong high pressure. Given the \nlight winds and clear skies, frost is looking probable for mainly \ncentral KS early Saturday.\n\nSUNDAY THROUGH NEXT WEEK...Model consensus supports a quick return \nto seasonable or even above normal temperatures, especially by next \nweek, as another deep upper trough sets up over the western CONUS.",
